 "record": {
  "scenario": "D4",
  "kind": "durable",
  "labels": {
   "purpose": "The user says Fahrenheit means little to them and they think in Celsius, and asks for the numbers again.",
   "preference": "The user thinks in Celsius: give temperatures in degrees Celsius, not (only) Fahrenheit.",
   "expected_scope": "global",
   "polarity": "Celsius first or only. A fact that says to use Fahrenheit, or that limits Celsius to this brewing recipe, is wrong or too narrow.",
   "acknowledged_if": "The second answer recognises the request and gives the temperatures in Celsius.",
   "probe_applied_if": "The probe answer gives the temperatures in degrees Celsius, either alone or first with Fahrenheit secondary.",
   "probe_partial_if": "The answer gives Fahrenheit first and Celsius only as a secondary conversion."
  },
  "prompts": {
   "turn1": "What fermentation temperature range does the recipe in brew.md call for, and is my garage, which sits at 61 degrees, okay for it?",
   "turn2": "Honestly, Fahrenheit doesn't mean much to me. I grew up with Celsius and that's what I think in. Could you give me those numbers again, and also tell me the mash temperature?"
  },
  "answers": {
   "turn1": "The recipe in `brew.md` (Hazel Porter) calls for fermenting between **62 °F and 68 °F** for 14 days.\n\nYour garage at 61 °F is 1 °F below the bottom of that range, so it's slightly too cold. It's close, but a few things could go wrong:\n\n- **Slow or stalled fermentation:** The yeast may work sluggishly and leave the beer under-attenuated.\n- **Cold nights:** If the garage drops further overnight, the risk goes up.\n- **Pitching:** The recipe pitches at 64 °F, so the wort would need to start warmer than the garage and will cool toward 61 °F.\n\nSome easy fixes:\n\n- Wrap the fermenter in a blanket or towel, or set it on an insulating surface such as foam board.\n- Use a heat wrap or a seedling heat mat with a thermostat controller.\n- Put it in a warmer spot, such as a closet indoors.\n\nFermentation also generates a little heat of its own, so the beer itself usually runs a few degrees above room temperature during the active phase. That may bring it into range. A stick-on thermometer on the fermenter will tell you the actual beer temperature.",
   "turn2": "Here are the numbers in Celsius, rounded to one decimal:\n\n- **Fermentation range:** 62 to 68 °F is **16.7 to 20 °C**, for 14 days.\n- **Your garage:** 61 °F is **16.1 °C**, about 0.6 °C below the bottom of the range. That's slightly cool, so the earlier advice still applies: insulate the fermenter or add gentle warmth.\n- **Mash:** 152 °F is **66.7 °C**, held for 60 minutes.\n\nThe recipe's other temperatures are:\n\n- **Pitch the yeast:** 64 °F is **17.8 °C**.\n- **Cold crash:** 35 °F is **1.7 °C**, for 2 days before bottling."
  },
  "base_changes": [],
